AI Agents are AI systems that autonomously plan and take multi-step actions using tools to achieve a goal — not just answering once, but observing results and deciding next steps on their own. 
What you built today (Claude + Kubernetes/GitHub/Jenkins MCP servers) is a real example — Claude acts as the "brain," MCP tools as the "hands."

An AI agent doesn't just generate text output — it takes real actions using tools (APIs, MCP servers, scripts) to actually do something in the real world: query a database, create an EC2 instance, 
restart a pod, open a GitHub PR, etc.

Most practical/interview-relevant framing for your work specifically:

"Today's AI agents (like Claude with MCP, or GitHub Copilot Workspace) are best understood as LLM-based agentic systems — they combine an LLM's reasoning with tool access to autonomously plan and 
execute tasks, which is a more modern evolution beyond the classic 'goal-based' or 'utility-based' agent categories from traditional AI theory."

1. Simple Reflex Agents

• Acts only on the current input, no memory of past actions — pure "if-this-then-that" reaction.
• Example: A thermostat — "if temp > threshold, turn on AC" — no learning, no memory.

2. Model-Based Reflex Agents

• Keeps an internal "model" of the world, so it accounts for things it can't directly observe right now.
• Example: A robot vacuum that remembers the room layout, avoiding areas it already cleaned.

3. Goal-Based Agents

• Doesn't just react — considers future outcomes and picks actions that move toward a specific goal.
• Example: A navigation system choosing the best route toward a destination, considering multiple paths.

4. Utility-Based Agents

• Like goal-based, but also weighs trade-offs — picks the action that maximizes overall "value/utility," not just any path to the goal.
• Example: Choosing a route that balances speed AND cost AND safety, not just the shortest path.

5. Learning Agents

• Improves its performance over time based on feedback/experience — this is where Reinforcement Learning agents (like AlphaGo) fit.
• Example: AlphaGo improving its strategy after each game played.

6. LLM-based / Agentic AI (the modern category — most relevant today)

• Uses an LLM as the "reasoning brain," combined with tool access, to autonomously plan and execute multi-step tasks.
• Example: Claude + Kubernetes + GitHub + Jenkins + AWS MCP servers.

